# Copy to .env and fill in. Everything here is optional: with no .env at all
# the CLI runs `--demo`, the API serves, and the page renders pasted scripts in
# local mode without calling a model at all.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# A shell `export` wins over this file, so one value can be overridden for a
# single run without editing anything. The file is found from wherever a
# command is started, searching upward to the repository root.

# --------------------------------------------------------------- model access
#
# Needed only when something has to be *written*: a topic becoming a tutorial,
# or pasted prose being structured. Rendering never calls a model, so local
# mode with lesson JSON or a script costs nothing and needs none of these.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- free models
#
# Gemini's free tier is 20 requests per model per day, so a spent allowance is
# the ordinary way a long day goes, not a fault. When it is gone the job
# carries on with the next provider below that has a key.
#
# ANY ONE OF THESE IS ENOUGH. They are tried in this order, and a provider
# with no key is skipped rather than tried and failed.
#
#   Groq     https://console.groq.com/keys
#   Hugging  https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens   (a "read" token is fine)
#   Router   https://openrouter.ai/keys               (the :free models cost $0)
#
# Paste the key after the = and restart the worker. Nothing else changes.

# Tried first, and best at this prompt. 20 requests per day per model on the
# free tier.  https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Free fallbacks, tried in this order when the one above is spent. Any one is
# enough, and the chain only reports a failure once every provider with a key
# has been tried.
#   https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=
#   https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens   (HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY also works)
HF_TOKEN=
#   https://openrouter.ai/keys   (the ":free" models cost nothing)
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Free tiers retire and rename models constantly, so each list can be corrected
# without a release. Comma-separated, tried in order; the first is the default.
# Shipped today:
#   Groq       openai/gpt-oss-120b, openai/gpt-oss-20b
#   HF         meta-llama/Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct
#   OpenRouter nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free
# GROQ_MODELS=
# HF_MODELS=
# OPENROUTER_MODELS=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- voices
#
# Kokoro runs locally at no per-character cost and is what the free tier uses.
# ElevenLabs is the better voice and the only part of this that bills: roughly
# $0.65-1.30 per lesson, against a free allowance of 10,000 characters.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ sign-in
#
# Both sides or neither. Configure the API and the web app together, or the
# page sends a development header the server has stopped honouring and every
# request is a 401.
#
# On the API: the project URL is enough -- the public signing keys are found
# under it. Supabase issues ES256 tokens now; SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET is only for
# a project created before that change.
# SUPABASE_URL=
# SUPABASE_JWKS_URL=
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=
# SUPABASE_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated
#
# The same two values again, on the web app (`web/.env.local`). Both are public
# by design -- the anon key is constrained by row-level security, not secrecy.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the service

# Postgres. The API and worker share this; the worker owns the schema.
# VIDEOSYNX_DSN=postgresql:///videosynx

# Where rendered videos are written when there is no object store.
# VIDEOSYNX_ARTIFACTS=/tmp/videosynx/artifacts

# Object storage instead of local disk. R2_BUCKET being unset is what selects
# the local backend, so setting only the others does nothing.
# R2_BUCKET=
# R2_ENDPOINT=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Signed artifact URLs are authorised by a query signature rather than a
# header, because a <video> element cannot send one. With no secret set a new
# one is generated at startup, so links stop working across a restart.
# VIDEOSYNX_URL_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ billing

# Needs a live key. Everything after checkout is tested with locally-signed
# webhook payloads; only the checkout itself is unproven.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Maps a Stripe price to a tier: {"price_abc": "paid"}
# STRIPE_PRICE_TIERS={}
# STRIPE_DEFAULT_TIER=paid
# STRIPE_SUCCESS_URL=
# STRIPE_CANCEL_URL=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- examples
#
# Which rendered tutorials the public landing page shows. A comma-separated
# list of job ids, and empty by default.
#
# A written list rather than a query on purpose: a user's video becoming
# public because it happened to be recent would be a privacy bug dressed as a
# feature. Publishing one is a decision someone makes about a particular
# video.
#
# The still on each card is made from the video itself, once, and kept.
# VIDEOSYNX_EXAMPLES=
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------- web

# Where the Next server forwards /api. Read by the server at startup and never
# sent to the browser, so changing it needs a restart and not a rebuild.
# VIDEOSYNX_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Which account the page claims to be while no sign-in is configured. Ignored
# the moment SUPABASE_URL or SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET is set on the API.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_USER=web-user:free

# Browsers allowed to call the API directly. The page goes through its own
# /api rewrite, so this is only for a client calling the API from elsewhere.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
